Juggling model selection and p-values is tricky regardless of what kind of model you are fitting. Part of the inference for the top-ranked model will be acknowledging the uncertainty in individual parameter estimates which will be reflected in the p-values. Building good figures always helps with this. It sounds like this is generally the approach you followed.

Case 1:
I would avoid trying to making comparisons between individual pairs of models post-hoc based on significance.  Not every p-value will necessarily be significant at p=0.05 in a model with low AIC. I'd just focus on the top-ranked model and acknowledge the uncertainty in the interaction term.

Cases 2-4:  I think your conclusions are reasonable.

Others may have more helpful opinions to add here.

Ken

